===== DEFAULT VALUES =====

In the class the turret is used in, set kP to 0.02, kI and kD to 0, lookaheadTime to 0.10,
POSITION_TOLERANCE to ~1.5, MIN_POWER to ~0.05 and UNWIND_POWER to 0.5. Then set integral and
lastError to 0;

===== SETTING TURRET LIMITS =====

To get started, run the encoderTester opMode, upon connecting a controller, use dpadUp to increase the
turret motor's position, respectively use dpadDown to decrease the position.

Keep moving the turret to one direction, until the robot's cables get stuck, then keep track of
the value shown in telemetry. Do the same for the opposite direction.

Stick these values into the MAX_TURRET_TICKS and MIN_TURRET_TICKS and apply a ~50 ticks (depending
on the motor's encoder resolution) safety margin.

===== PID TUNING =====

Tuning must be done while ROBOT IS MOVING, run the turretTuning opMode

I. Proportional
First, set the kI and kD variables to 0 if not already set.
Gradually increase kP until turret moves fast towards target.
In case the turret overshoots, reduce kP.

II. Derivative
Start with a small kD. (~0.001)
Increase until overshoot is reduced.
Reduce in case of oscillations/jitter near the target.

III. Integral
Only add if there's a steady offset.
Increase slowly until offset disappears.
Reduce if slow oscillations or instability occurs.

===== PREDICTIVE TRACKING =====

Tune the lookaheadTime while the robot moves:

If the turret lags behind, this could mean the lookaheadTime is too low
If the turret overshoots, reduce lookaheadTime

Typical lookaheadTime values are ~0.08-0.14 seconds

